usb: dwc2: no SOF interrupt in descriptor-DMA mode; adaptive root-port speed (cumulative)

Cumulative diff from the 6.12 BSP baseline (the linux-esp32-s31 submodule)
to the current 7.1 state of drivers/usb/dwc2, in the same form as 0023-0025.
It supersedes the dwc2 hunks of 0018 and 0019 (kept for history).
Regenerate with:

    for f in $(cd linux-71-port && git -C ../linux-esp32-s31 ls-files drivers/usb/dwc2 2>/dev/null || ls drivers/usb/dwc2); do
        diff -u linux-esp32-s31/$f linux-71-port/$f --label a/$f --label b/$f
    done

The new part: dwc2_hcd_qh_add() enables GINTSTS_SOF when the first
periodic QH appears, and dwc2_hcd_qh_unlink() never clears it in
descriptor-DMA mode, while dwc2_sof_intr() has nothing to walk in that
mode (the periodic schedule is the hardware frame list, and
dwc2_hcd_start_xfer_ddma() reads the frame number itself). Every board
therefore ran a 1 kHz SOF interrupt - 8 kHz at high speed - for nothing.

Measured 2026-09-03 on the ESP32-S31 (root port forced to full speed, a
Logitech receiver and an 8BitDo receiver behind a hub, descriptor DMA):
masking the bit by hand took idle USB interrupts from 1,039/s to 0 with
control and interrupt-IN transfers unaffected (HID rebind clean, 413 input
events at 4 ms average lag), and cpubench 66.3-67.3 -> 70.3-71.0 M/s,
about 6% of the core. The enable is now gated on a module parameter,
sof_irq (default off), so the old behaviour is one boot argument away.

Adaptive root-port speed (2026-09-04): the port starts at high speed with
descriptor DMA. The first queue head that needs split transactions (a
full/low-speed device behind a high-speed hub - which this core cannot
carry in descriptor mode, see patches/0027) is refused and the port is
dropped to full speed: FSLSSupp set, port power bounced for 200 ms, the
bus re-enumerates as plain full speed. When an endpoint later goes away
the port tries high speed again after 3 s (ignoring the storm the switch
itself causes for 10 s). host_full_speed=Y pins full speed as before;
auto_speed=0 disables the switching. Measured on the first boot: hub at
high speed at 1.8 s, drop at 2.9 s, both receivers up at 7.1 s, rcS at
8.2 s - no boot-time cost against the pinned build.

